# The site, the third base element: one of a dock's twenty-four directions, each pointing out through one face to the
# neighbor on the other side, where a single tone lives. The twenty-four sites pair into twelve lines, a site and its
# opposite, and the line, not the single site, is what the knit's collision acts on.
#
# This file MODELS the pairing. A direction's orientation is its two signs (a D4 root has two nonzero slots, each plus or
# minus). The opposite direction flips both signs. The opposite map is proved an INVOLUTION (opposite of opposite is the
# original) and FIXED-POINT-FREE (no direction is its own opposite), which is exactly what makes the twenty-four sites
# pair cleanly into twelve lines with none left over. The geometry's count 24 = 6*4 lives in `forced-dock-24-cell`.

# A sign, the orientation of one nonzero slot of a direction.
form sign
  case plus
  case minus

# The flip of a sign.
task flip-sign
  take s, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read s
    case plus
      send back
        make minus
    case minus
      send back
        make plus

# A way, a direction's orientation: the two signs of its two nonzero slots.
form way
  case pair
    link one, like sign
    link two, like sign

# The opposite direction: flip both signs. This is the antipodal site across the dock, the other end of the line.
task opposite
  take w, like way
  like way
  fork case, read w
    case pair
      link one
      link two
      send back
        make pair
          bind one
            call flip-sign
              read one
          bind two
            call flip-sign
              read two

# A truth value, to test whether two ways are the same.
form flag
  case yes
  case no

# Are two signs equal?
task same-sign
  take a, like sign
  take b, like sign
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case plus
      fork case, read b
        case plus
          send back
            make yes
        case minus
          send back
            make no
    case minus
      fork case, read b
        case plus
          send back
            make no
        case minus
          send back
            make yes

# Does a way equal another way? Equal on both signs.
task same-way
  take a, like way
  take b, like way
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case pair
      link one
      link two
      fork case, read b
        case pair
          link three
          link four
          send back
            call meet-flag
              call same-sign
                read one
                read three
              call same-sign
                read two
                read four

# conjunction on flags.
task meet-flag
  take a, like flag
  take b, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      send back
        make no

# The flip of a sign is never the sign itself: a sign and its flip differ. The seed of fixed-point-freeness.
rule flip-sign-differs
  mark s, like sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-sign
        read s
        call flip-sign
          read s
      make no
  fold s

# The opposite map is an INVOLUTION: opposite (opposite w) = w, for every direction. The other end of the line points
# back. Proved by case analysis, using that flipping a sign twice returns it.
rule opposite-is-an-involution
  mark w, like way
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call opposite
        call opposite
          read w
      read w
  fold w

# The opposite is FIXED-POINT-FREE: no direction is its own opposite, same-way w (opposite w) = no, for every direction.
# Because the opposite flips both signs and a flipped sign always differs. This is what guarantees the twenty-four sites
# pair into twelve genuine lines, with none its own partner.
rule opposite-has-no-fixed-point
  mark w, like way
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-way
        read w
        call opposite
          read w
      make no
  fold w
